  notification system answers it one of three ways:

  - [1mImplicit only[0m — you're subscribed to a task because of your relationship to it: you created it, you're assigned, you
    commented. Zero UI, zero user effort, and new users get useful notifications on day one. The cost is that people
    can't opt out of a noisy task or opt into one they merely want to observe, and "why am I getting this?" becomes a
    support question.
  - [1mExplicit only[0m — a watch/star button. Perfectly predictable and easy to explain, but it starts empty: assign someone
    a task and they hear nothing until they think to watch it. In practice teams find this broken.
  - [1mImplicit with explicit override[0m — relationships auto-subscribe you, and a watch/mute control lets you add or remove
    yourself. This is what GitHub, Linear, and Jira all converged on, and the convergence isn't an accident.

  The storage difference is real: implicit-only needs no subscription table at all (you derive recipients from the task
  row at send time), while the override model needs a [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m table with an explicit muted state — and
  retrofitting that later means backfilling rows for every existing relationship.

  My recommendation: [1mimplicit with explicit override[0m. The extra cost now is one table and one button; the cost of adding
  it later is a migration plus a backfill, and mute is the first thing anyone asks for once notifications actually
  start arriving.
